  "evidence": "TEXT_G: Balance the structural current budget by 2017/18 with spending growth after balance; raise personal allowance; anti-avoidance and non-dom restrictions; entry/exit border checks and faster asylum decisions; defence SDSR with single security budget, invest in cyber and readiness, reduce (not end) nuclear patrol posture; require qualified teachers and a core curriculum; NHS England +£8bn and mental-health waiting-time standards. TEXT_C: Restore 50p top rate, bankers’ bonus tax, mansion tax, abolish non-dom; uprate benefits by cost of living, abolish Bedroom Tax, halt/review UC and PIP rollout; raise minimum wage to £8.70, expand union powers and employee board representation; oppose renewal of the UK nuclear deterrent and refocus defence on northern/Arctic needs; extensive devolution toward full financial responsibility and a ‘double‑majority’ for any UK exit; protect NHS from privatisation and expand mental health; fracking moratorium.",
  "reasoning": "Given the electorate’s higher weighting on authority, loyalty, and proportionality, TEXT_G aligns better by emphasizing fiscal discipline, border enforcement, maintaining national defence capacity, and institutional standards. TEXT_C foregrounds equality and care through redistribution, welfare expansion, devolution, and disarmament, which map less closely to the profile’s top priorities.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_G"
}
